  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\nA warm front will lift north into our area tonight ahead of a low\npressure system riding east along it. This will be followed by a \nstrong cold front that will push southeast across our area through \nFriday morning. \n\nGood moisture transport will develop across the region in the \ndeveloping southwest flow through this evening. As the warm front \nlifts north into our area and the low approaches, shear profiles \nwill be on the increase. However, there remains some question as to \nhow much instability will develop/be advected up into our area ahead\nof this. As of now, it looks like the best chance for some \ndestabilization will be across our far west/southwest. This is where\nthe best chance for any severe storms seems to be this evening. If \nsome stronger storms are able to develop, damaging wind would be the \nprimary threat. With high PWs, locally heavy rainfall will be \npossible with any stronger storms, but this threat too looks to be \nlimited. \n\nBetween the warm front tonight and the cold front on Friday \nmorning, we will have non-diurnal temperatures over the next 24 \nhours. Temperatures will remain cool through much of this afternoon \nbefore slowly rising overnight in developing southwest low level \nflow. Temperatures will then rapidly drop off from the northwest \nThursday morning as strong low level CAA overspreads the area.\n\nKey Message 2) \nIn the low level CAA, temperatures will level off/continue to drop \nthrough the day on Friday with readings by late afternoon only in the \nmid 50s across the area. Gusty winds will also develop in the CAA \nwith wind gusts in the 25 to 35 mph range possible through the day. \n\nThe cold air will then remain in place across our area through the \nweekend. Saturday night looks to be the coldest night, with clear \nskies and light winds. Will undercut guidance a bit with lows in the\nlow to mid 30s and widespread frost expected.",
